.TH ncheck "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
ncheck \- print file names corresponding to i-numbers
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBncheck [\-as] [\-i \fIn ...\fB] [\fIfilesystem ...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
ncheck -- print file names corresponding to i-numbers
Usage:	ncheck [ options ] [ filesystem ... ]
Options:
	-a	Print names including '.' and '..'
	-i n... Only print names for listed i-numbers 'n...'
	-s	Print only special files and files with setuid mode
.HE
An i-number identifies an i-node.
.B ncheck
prints, for each named
.IR filesystem ,
a list of the path names that lead to each i-node in that file
system; each line gives an i-number followed by one of the path
names by which it can be reached.
With no
.I filesystem
argument,
.B ncheck
checks
.BR /dev/rrm00 .
.PP
.B ncheck
first scans the whole i-list to find which i-numbers are
directories, then scans every directory to build the path from each
directory to its entries, and finally prints a name for every
i-node (or, with
.BR \-i ,
only for the listed i-numbers; or, with
.BR \-s ,
only for special files and files with the set-user-id or
set-group-id bit on).
By default, entries named
.B .
and
.B ..
are not printed;
.B \-a
prints them too.
Names ending in
.B /.
identify directories.
.IP \fB\-a\fR
Include entries named
.B .
and
.B ..
in the output.
.IP "\fB\-i\ \fIn ...\fR"
Print only the names of the listed i-numbers, instead of every
i-node in the file system.
Up to 20 numbers may be given, either after one
.B \-i
or repeated; the list ends at the first non-numeric argument.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Print only the names of block-special, character-special, set-user-id,
and set-group-id files; useful for a quick security check of a file
system.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Accepted but currently a no-op; reserved for a future search for
orphaned (unreferenced) structure.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B ncheck
marks a directory entry whose i-number is out of range as
.BR bad ,
and represents a loop found while tracing a path back to the root
with
.BR "..." .
It prints a message and sets a nonzero exit status if a file system
cannot be opened, its super block looks implausible, or an i-node
block cannot be read.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR check ,
.BR dcheck ,
.B icheck
